Drop a scroll region a resize leaves no room for

on_resize() clamps the scroll region's bottom (and right margin) to the
new size and leaves its top (and left margin) where they were. Shrunk
below the region's top, the region is upside down, and the next line
feed calls scroll() with a negative height: memmove() takes it as an
enormous size and the process crashes. Any program that scrolls part of
the screen sets a region -- vim, less, tmux -- so making the window
small under one was enough. Found by fuzzing BeerSSH's emulator under
ASan.

Reset a region with no room left to the whole screen, as before any was
set; the program sets a new one for the size it now has.

--- a/src/state.c
+++ b/src/state.c
@@ -2074,6 +2074,21 @@
   if(state->scrollregion_right > -1)
     UBOUND(state->scrollregion_right, state->cols);
 
+  /* A region the new size leaves no room in is no region. Clamping its
+   * bottom alone left its top below it -- set by any program that scrolls
+   * part of the screen, vim, less, tmux -- and the next line feed moved a
+   * negative number of rows, which memmove() took as an enormous one.
+   * The whole screen scrolls instead until the program sets a region for
+   * the size it now has. */
+  if(state->scrollregion_top >= SCROLLREGION_BOTTOM(state)) {
+    state->scrollregion_top = 0;
+    state->scrollregion_bottom = -1;
+  }
+  if(state->scrollregion_left >= SCROLLREGION_RIGHT(state)) {
+    state->scrollregion_left = 0;
+    state->scrollregion_right = -1;
+  }
+
   VTermStateFields fields = {
     .pos       = state->pos,
     .lineinfos = { [0] = state->lineinfos[0], [1] = state->lineinfos[1] },
